  "Number": 9,
  "Question": "The two legs of a right triangle, which are altitudes, have lengths \\(2\\sqrt3\\) and \\(6.\\) How long is the third altitude of the triangle?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The third altitude is the perpendicular from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse. To find it, compute the same triangle's area using two different bases.\n\nLet \\(c>0\\) be the hypotenuse and \\(h>0\\) the desired altitude. By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nc=\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt3)^2+6^2}\n=\\sqrt{48}=4\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nThe legs are perpendicular, so they form a base-height pair. Thus the area is\n\\[\n\\frac12(2\\sqrt3)(6)=6\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nUsing the hypotenuse as the base gives the same area:\n\\[\n\\frac12(4\\sqrt3)h=6\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(2\\sqrt3>0\\) yields \\(h=3\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{3}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
